WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.815
The term, wireless bridging, can actually have multiple meanings, though the main idea of a wireless bridge is to connect two segments, often wired, of a network.

00:08.815 --> 00:14.669
For example, you could have a large warehouse room to where several devices could connect to one wireless access point

00:14.669 --> 00:20.754
with the signal traveling across the warehouse to another wireless access point with several devices connected to it.

00:20.754 --> 00:27.517
And, if the wireless access points are in bridge mode, networks with different 802.11 standards can connect to each other.

00:27.517 --> 00:34.624
Another definition of a wireless bridge is that it can serve as a repeater, that is, it can boost a signal that is reaching its maximum distance.

00:34.624 --> 00:40.539
Come to think of it, that is what a wired repeater, known as a hub, does as well. It boosts a signal.

00:40.539 --> 00:47.191
If you take away the cables, wired and wireless networks really have quite a few similarities.

